2cecb077 (705 1793273644808 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f379") (src . "landing-freeze: --tree compares the freeze with the tested edit directory\n\nA landing freeze froze whatever drafts the writer held, and nothing tied\nthem to the directory that was tested: after a refused edit-sync (which by\ndesign writes nothing), an edit never synced, a sync from another export,\nor a half-finished sync, the freeze held content other than the tree that\npassed. landing-freeze (and its --check) takes --tree <edit-dir>: the\ndirectory's sidecar must name this store and this writer, and after the\nfreeze's tree is written from the one view it read, every file of it is\ncompared byte for byte with the edit directory, dot-named entries skipped\non both sides. Any difference refuses tree-differs, naming the files that\ndiffer and those on one side only, and says how to make them agree.\nEvery non-dot file of the edit directory counts, so scratch files and\nreadings belong elsewhere. A trailing slash is accepted as the other edit\ntools accept it; a bare --tree, or a directory spelled with a final \".\"\nor \"..\", is a usage error before anything runs. Without --tree the\nbehaviour is as before.\n\n- tools/landing-freeze.sc: --tree for the freeze and the check.\n- tools/edit-sync.sc: its header says a refused sync leaves the drafts as\n  they were, which a freeze would then freeze.\n- README: the landing section says so.\n- Tests: edit-tools (a refused sync then a freeze, a resync, an unsynced\n  edit, another writer's export, the check, trailing slashes, a bare\n  --tree, a final \".\" or \"..\"); unreadable-census; rows-baseline.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 510))))
